  "description": "Change of use from Use Class C3 (dwelling house) to Use Class C4 (HMO-house in multiple occupation) for 6 persons, with refuse and cycle storage, rear dormer and rear outrigger dormer with front roof lights.",
  "epc_number": null,
  "ward": "Upper Edmonton",
  "last_updated": "2026-11-19T21:10:01.419Z",
  "street_name": "Dysons Road",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "The site lies within Flood Zone 2 and an area at risk of fluvial and surface water flooding. The submitted Flood Risk Assessment and proposed mitigation measures fail to demonstrate that the development would be safe throughout its lifetime. In particular, the proposed changes to the ground-floor levels do not adequately address the flood risk to the proposed ground floor sleeping accommodation or provide the required clearance above the modelled flood level. The proposal is therefore contrary to Policy CP28 of the Core Strategy (2010), Policies DMD59, DMD60 and DMD62 of the Development Management Document (2014), Policies SI12 and SI13 of the London Plan (2021), and the relevant flood-risk provisions of the National Planning Policy Framework (2026).",
    "The proposed development would fail to provide high quality, functional and inclusive accommodation for six occupiers. Bedroom 6 would be constrained by its sloping roof, resulting in a poorly usable bedroom and en-suite, while the cookers proximity to the internal exit would create an unsatisfactory means of escape arrangement. Access to the rear cycle store through the narrow kitchen and over internal and rear steps would conflict with cooking activities and be impractical and potentially unsafe. The raised floors and steps introduced for flood mitigation would also create an inaccessible layout, particularly for people with mobility impairments. Taken cumulatively, the proposal would provide an unacceptable standard of accommodation and inadequate cycle access, contrary to Policies D5, D6 and T5 of the London Plan (2021) and Policies DMD5, DMD8, DMD37, DMD45 and DMD47 of the Development Management Document (2014)."
  ],
